<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Echota</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">New Echota was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> Nation from 1825 until removal in the 1830s, near modern Calhoun in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Laid out as a planned town with a council house, a supreme court and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> office, it published the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee-phoenix">Cherokee Phoenix</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-new-echota">Treaty of New Echota</a> was signed there in 1835, and the site was later used to hold Cherokee before removal; it is now a state historic site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-echota.jpg" alt="New Echota" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomson200 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Council_House,_New_Echota,_GA_July_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>New Echota lies in present-day Gordon County, north of Calhoun and south of Resaca, at the confluence of the Coosawattee and Conasauga rivers, which join to form the Oostanaula River. Indigenous peoples had lived at the site for thousands of years before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a>, who knew it as Gansagi or Gansagiyi. They renamed it in 1825 after their former chief town of Chota on the lower Little Tennessee River; English speakers often called it Newtown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before New Echota the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> council had sat at nearby Ustanali, from 1788. The government was meeting at New Echota by 1819, and on 12 November 1825 the Cherokee Nation designated it the capital. The council built a two-storey Council House and a Supreme Court, and later a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> office for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee-phoenix">Cherokee Phoenix</a>, the first Native American newspaper, edited by Elias Boudinot and printed in English and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sequoyah">Sequoyah</a>'s syllabary with type cast by the missionary Samuel Worcester. Council meetings filled the town with several hundred Cherokee.</p><p>After the Indian Removal Act, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> land in its Sixth Land Lottery in 1832, and the Georgia Guard evicted Cherokee from their properties; by 1834 the town was emptying and the council had moved to Red Clay. On 29 December 1835 a minority group, the Treaty Party, signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-new-echota">Treaty of New Echota</a> in Boudinot's house, and the Senate ratified it despite the objections of John Ross, the principal chief. In 1838 the US Army under Winfield Scott held Cherokee at Fort Wool, a camp at New Echota, before their removal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site lay abandoned for more than a century. Excavations from 1954 found remains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> town and of earlier cultures, including type used to print the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee-phoenix">Cherokee Phoenix</a>. Georgia rebuilt the Council House, the Supreme Court and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> office, restored the Worcester House and opened the site as a historical park in 1962. It was designated a National Historic Landmark in 1973.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “New Echota” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the late 1820s the result was a republic with written laws, a capital at <b>New Echota</b> and a constitution.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cherokee-nation">Cherokee Nation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>US commissioners and a small minority faction of Cherokee signed a treaty at <b>New Echota</b>, Georgia, ceding all Cherokee lands east of the Mississippi.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-29">29 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cherokee Phoenix appeared in English and Cherokee at <b>New Echota</b>, capital of the Cherokee Nation: the first newspaper published by Native Americans and the first in a Native American language.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-21">21 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
